{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"grep -n 'conf.*[Aa]dvance\\\\|[Aa]dvance.*conf\\\\|[Aa]pplyConfChange\\\\|[Cc]onfig' ../agent-source/doc.go | head -40; sed -n '30,145p' ../agent-source/doc.go; sed -n '90,128p' ../agent-source/rawnode.go; grep -n 'ReadIndex\\\\|pendingReadIndex' ../agent-source/raft_test.go | tail -30; grep -R -n 'DisableConfChangeValidation\\\\|StepDownOnRemoval' ../agent-source/*.go | head -30\"","aggregated_output":"36:\tc := &Config{\n56:\tc := &Config{\n94:If any committed Entry has Type EntryConfChange, call Node.ApplyConfChange()\n95:to apply it to the node. The configuration change may be cancelled at this point\n96:by setting the NodeID field to zero before calling ApplyConfChange\n97:(but ApplyConfChange must be called one way or the other, and the decision to cancel\n138:\t        s.Node.ApplyConfChange(cc)\n160:After config change is committed, some committed entry with type\n165:\tn.ApplyConfChange(cc)\n174:The library can be configured with an alternate interface for local storage\n177:AsynchronousStorageWrites, and can be enabled using the flag on the Config\n250:\t          s.Node.ApplyConfChange(cc)\n270:since the old and new configurations are guaranteed to overlap.\n349:\tprotocol. When Config.PreVote is true, a pre-election is carried out first\nThe primary object in raft is a Node. You either start a Node from scratch\nusing raft.StartNode or start a Node from some initial state using raft.RestartNode.\n\nTo start a node from scratch:\n\n\tstorage := raft.NewMemoryStorage()\n\tc := &Config{\n\t  ID:              0x01,\n\t  ElectionTick:    10,\n\t  HeartbeatTick:   1,\n\t  Storage:         storage,\n\t  MaxSizePerMsg:   4096,\n\t  MaxInflightMsgs: 256,\n\t}\n\tn := raft.StartNode(c, []raft.Peer{{ID: 0x02}, {ID: 0x03}})\n\nTo restart a node from previous state:\n\n\tstorage := raft.NewMemoryStorage()\n\n\t// recover the in-memory storage from persistent\n\t// snapshot, state and entries.\n\tstorage.ApplySnapshot(snapshot)\n\tstorage.SetHardState(state)\n\tstorage.Append(entries)\n\n\tc := &Config{\n\t  ID:              0x01,\n\t  ElectionTick:    10,\n\t  HeartbeatTick:   1,\n\t  Storage:         storage,\n\t  MaxSizePerMsg:   4096,\n\t  MaxInflightMsgs: 256,\n\t}\n\n\t// restart raft without peer information.\n\t// peer information is already included in the storage.\n\tn := raft.RestartNode(c)\n\nNow that you are holding onto a Node you have a few responsibilities:\n\nFirst, you must read from the Node.Ready() channel and process the updates\nit contains. These steps may be performed in parallel, except as noted in step\n2.\n\n1. Write HardState, Entries, and Snapshot to persistent storage if they are\nnot empty. Note that when writing an Entry with Index i, any\npreviously-persisted entries with Index >= i must be discarded.\n\n2. Send all Messages to the nodes named in the To field. It is important that\nno messages be sent until the latest HardState has been persisted to disk,\nand all Entries written by any previous Ready batch (Messages may be sent while\nentries from the same batch are being persisted). To reduce the I/O latency, an\noptimization can be applied to make leader write to disk in parallel with its\nfollowers (as explained at section 10.2.1 in Raft thesis). If any Message has type\nMsgSnap, call Node.ReportSnapshot() after it has been sent (these messages may be\nlarge).\n\nNote: Marshalling messages is not thread-safe; it is important that you\nmake sure that no new entries are persisted while marshalling.\nThe easiest way to achieve this is to serialize the messages directly inside\nyour main raft loop.\n\n3. Apply Snapshot (if any) and CommittedEntries to the state machine.\nIf any committed Entry has Type EntryConfChange, call Node.ApplyConfChange()\nto apply it to the node. The configuration change may be cancelled at this point\nby setting the NodeID field to zero before calling ApplyConfChange\n(but ApplyConfChange must be called one way or the other, and the decision to cancel\nmust be based solely on the state machine and not external information such as\nthe observed health of the node).\n\n4. Call Node.Advance() to signal readiness for the next batch of updates.\nThis may be done at any time after step 1, although all updates must be processed\nin the order they were returned by Ready.\n\nSecond, all persisted log entries must be made available via an\nimplementation of the Storage interface. The provided MemoryStorage\ntype can be used for this (if you repopulate its state upon a\nrestart), or you can supply your own disk-backed implementation.\n\nThird, when you receive a message from another node, pass it to Node.Step:\n\n\tfunc recvRaftRPC(ctx context.Context, m raftpb.Message) {\n\t\tn.Step(ctx, m)\n\t}\n\nFinally, you need to call Node.Tick() at regular intervals (probably\nvia a time.Ticker). Raft has two important timeouts: heartbeat and the\nelection timeout. However, internally to the raft package time is\nrepresented by an abstract \"tick\".\n\nThe total state machine handling loop will look something like this:\n\n\tfor {\n\t  select {\n\t  case <-s.Ticker:\n\t    n.Tick()\n\t  case rd := <-s.Node.Ready():\n\t    saveToStorage(rd.State, rd.Entries, rd.Snapshot)\n\t    send(rd.Messages)\n\t    if !raft.IsEmptySnap(rd.Snapshot) {\n\t      processSnapshot(rd.Snapshot)\n\t    }\n\t    for _, entry := range rd.CommittedEntries {\n\t      process(entry)\n\t      if entry.Type == raftpb.EntryConfChange {\n\t        var cc raftpb.ConfChange\n\t        cc.Unmarshal(entry.Data)\n\t        s.Node.ApplyConfChange(cc)\n\t      }\n\t    }\n\t    s.Node.Advance()\n\t  case <-s.done:\n\t    return\n\t  }\n\t}\nfunc (rn *RawNode) Propose(data []byte) error {\n\treturn rn.raft.Step(pb.Message{\n\t\tType: pb.MsgProp,\n\t\tFrom: rn.raft.id,\n\t\tEntries: []pb.Entry{\n\t\t\t{Data: data},\n\t\t}})\n}\n\n// ProposeConfChange proposes a config change. See (Node).ProposeConfChange for\n// details.\nfunc (rn *RawNode) ProposeConfChange(cc pb.ConfChangeI) error {\n\tm, err := confChangeToMsg(cc)\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn err\n\t}\n\treturn rn.raft.Step(m)\n}\n\n// ApplyConfChange applies a config change to the local node. The app must call\n// this when it applies a configuration change, except when it decides to reject\n// the configuration change, in which case no call must take place.\nfunc (rn *RawNode) ApplyConfChange(cc pb.ConfChangeI) *pb.ConfState {\n\tcs := rn.raft.applyConfChange(cc.AsV2())\n\treturn &cs\n}\n\n// Step advances the state machine using the given message.\nfunc (rn *RawNode) Step(m pb.Message) error {\n\t// Ignore unexpected local messages receiving over network.\n\tif IsLocalMsg(m.Type) && !IsLocalMsgTarget(m.From) {\n\t\treturn ErrStepLocalMsg\n\t}\n\tif IsResponseMsg(m.Type) && !IsLocalMsgTarget(m.From) && rn.raft.trk.Progress[m.From] == nil {\n\t\treturn ErrStepPeerNotFound\n\t}\n\treturn rn.raft.Step(m)\n}\n\n1193:// readOnly readIndexQueue and pendingReadIndex map.\n1205:\tsm.Step(pb.Message{From: 2, Type: pb.MsgReadIndex, Entries: []pb.Entry{{Data: ctx}}})\n1211:\trequire.Len(t, sm.readOnly.pendingReadIndex, 1)\n1212:\t_, ok := sm.readOnly.pendingReadIndex[string(ctx)]\n1220:\trequire.Empty(t, sm.readOnly.pendingReadIndex)\n1221:\t_, ok = sm.readOnly.pendingReadIndex[string(ctx)]\n1941:\t\tnt.send(pb.Message{From: tt.sm.id, To: tt.sm.id, Type: pb.MsgReadIndex, Entries: []pb.Entry{{Data: tt.wctx}}})\n1985:\t\tnt.send(pb.Message{From: tt.sm.id, To: tt.sm.id, Type: pb.MsgReadIndex, Entries: []pb.Entry{{Data: tt.wctx}}})\n2036:\t\tnt.send(pb.Message{From: tt.sm.id, To: tt.sm.id, Type: pb.MsgReadIndex, Entries: []pb.Entry{{Data: tt.wctx}}})\n2046:// TestReadOnlyForNewLeader ensures that a leader only accepts MsgReadIndex message\n2085:\tnt.send(pb.Message{From: 1, To: 1, Type: pb.MsgReadIndex, Entries: []pb.Entry{{Data: wctx}}})\n2106:\tnt.send(pb.Message{From: 1, To: 1, Type: pb.MsgReadIndex, Entries: []pb.Entry{{Data: wctx}}})\n../agent-source/raft.go:258:\t// DisableConfChangeValidation turns off propose-time verification of\n../agent-source/raft.go:278:\tDisableConfChangeValidation bool\n../agent-source/raft.go:280:\t// StepDownOnRemoval makes the leader step down when it is removed from the\n../agent-source/raft.go:285:\tStepDownOnRemoval bool\n../agent-source/raft.go:391:\t// disableConfChangeValidation is Config.DisableConfChangeValidation,\n../agent-source/raft.go:462:\t\tdisableConfChangeValidation: c.DisableConfChangeValidation,\n../agent-source/raft.go:463:\t\tstepDownOnRemoval:           c.StepDownOnRemoval,\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"agent_message","text":"The controller now records the joint-exit defect as confirmed in scope. The pending-read API explicitly permits lost requests and assigns retries to the caller, so the release asymmetry alone does not establish a progress violation.\n\nA separate safety-relevant premise emerged: the docs permit early `Advance` while application is still running, but election and configuration-proposal guards use the index that `Advance` updates. I’ll investigate whether that ordering can admit work under an unapplied voter configuration, retaining the stricter RawNode wording as conflicting evidence.\n"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat > early_candidate.py <<'PY'\nimport json\nm=json.load(open('../audit-spec/v2.json'))\nfor a in m['activities']:a.pop('behavior_ids',None)\nfor f in m['facts']:\n for k in ['established_by','consumed_by']:f.pop(k,None)\nB={b['id']:b for b in m['behaviors']}\nB['B-auto-exit']['unknowns']=[]\nB['B-auto-exit']['important_branches']=['If the internal exit proposal is rejected during transfer, later appliedTo calls can retry. Transfer abort only clears the obstruction; it does not generate an appliedTo trigger.']\nB['B-application']['cross_activity_effects']['A4']='Configuration application is a separate caller call. Normal loops perform ApplyConfChange before Advance, but Node.Advance documentation permits early notification while application is still running. appliedTo may therefore precede actual configuration application under that documented optimization.'\nB['B-application']['source_ids']+=['S-early-advance','S-doc-advance','S-raw-conf-contract']\nB['B-application']['unknowns']=['Does the documented early-Advance optimization include configuration entries, and what prevents raftLog.applied from satisfying configuration/election guards while ApplyConfChange is outstanding?']\nB['B-application']['produces_fact_ids']=['F-applied-notification']\nB['B-ready']['source_ids']+=['S-early-advance','S-doc-advance','S-raw-advance-contract']\nB['B-ready']['important_branches']+=['RawNode.Advance describes applied-and-saved progress, while Node.Advance explicitly allows notification during application; RawNode methods refer to Node documentation. This contract boundary needs investigation for configuration entries.']\nfor i in ['B-formation','B-authority','B-auto-exit']:\n B[i].setdefault('consumes_fact_ids',[]).append('F-applied-notification')\nB['B-recover']['produces_fact_ids'].append('F-applied-notification')\nm['facts'].append(dict(id='F-applied-notification',meaning='The raftLog.applied endpoint records application progress reported through configured Applied, storage application/snapshot completion, or synchronous Advance. It is consumed as an applied-prefix index by configuration admission and campaign guards. Node documentation permits reporting before all application work finishes; whether this includes outstanding configuration application is unresolved.',identity={'group':'Raft instance','node':'local node ID','endpoint':'reported applied index'},validity_context='The local log and caller completion protocol; not unconditionally proof that every ApplyConfChange through this index has executed.',representation=['raftLog.applied'],durability='Caller stores its applied position; raft does not persist this endpoint in HardState.',recovery='Config.Applied supplies restart application progress; restored snapshot completion advances the endpoint.',source_ids=['S-advance','S-step','S-init','S-early-advance','S-doc-advance','S-proposal','S-hup-scan'],unknowns=['Precise caller duty for configuration application relative to optimized Advance.']))\nB['B-config']['unknowns']=['Configuration-driven commit omits pending-read release. The API permits request loss and caller retries; no automatic per-request completion duty is yet grounded.']\nB['B-config']['source_ids'].append('S-read-contract')\nm['surfaces'][1]['reason']='ReadIndex may lose requests without notice and caller must retry. Missing release on configuration-driven commit remains an implementation asymmetry but is not by itself an established liveness breach; use a fresh context per documented version restrictions.'\nm['surfaces'][1]['source_ids'].append('S-read-contract')\nm['surfaces'].append(dict(entry_point='Node.Advance versus ApplyConfChange',disposition='mapped',behavior_ids=['B-application','B-ready','B-formation','B-authority'],reason='Early Advance is documented, while applied-index guards control new configuration proposals and campaigning. Investigate exact caller contract and possible safety histories.',source_ids=['S-early-advance','S-doc-advance','S-raw-advance-contract','S-proposal','S-hup-scan'],high_consequence=True))\nco=m['core_overview'];co['open_details']=['ReadIndex loss/retry contract limits any inference from configuration-driven release asymmetry','Pending read messages retained across reset','Early Advance notification versus actual configuration application and applied-index guards']\nco['connection']['explanation']+=' Automatic joint exit has an appliedTo-driven retry; transfer abort alone does not schedule that consumer. Separately, the documented early-Advance optimization means reported application progress and actual configuration application must be distinguished when interpreting admission guards.'\nco['connection']['source_ids']+=['S-early-advance','S-doc-advance','S-read-contract']\nchanges={}\nfor i in ['B-auto-exit','B-application','B-ready','B-formation','B-authority','B-recover','B-config']:\n changes[i]=dict(impact='dependency' if i\"'!='\"'B-auto-exit' else 'clarification',source_ids=B[i]['source_ids'],rationale='Record notification/consumption dependencies and the newly sourced early-Advance contract boundary; replace answered automatic-exit continuation unknown with its conditional mechanism.',preserves='C-auto-exit-continuation and its fixed witness explicitly apply the configuration before Advance, so their requirement, dependencies and observed closed-cycle scope remain valid. New uncertainty concerns a different caller ordering.')\nchanges['core_overview']=dict(impact='clarification',source_ids=['S-auto','S-abort','S-early-advance','S-read-contract'],rationale='Retain the conditional auto-exit relationship and read retry contract; navigate to a distinct application-notification boundary.',preserves='No change to the confirmed automatic-exit requirement or its legal prefix.')\nchanges['surface:raft.switchToConfig pending reads']=dict(impact='clarification',source_ids=['S-read-contract'],rationale='The sourced caller retry duty limits the suspected liveness obligation; no result is attributed to an unexecuted read scenario.',preserves='Independent of the automatic joint-exit witness.')\nq=dict(question='Can the documented early-Advance optimization let configuration/election guards treat a configuration entry as applied while its ApplyConfChange call is still pending, permitting decisions under stale voter eligibility?',importance='If legal completion ordering allows multiple configuration changes or elections under obsolete quorum membership, independent groups could commit conflicting log entries. That consequence is a hypothesis requiring a legal history, not a finding.',source_ids=['S-early-advance','S-doc-advance','S-raw-advance-contract','S-raw-boundary','S-raw-conf-contract','S-advance','S-step','S-proposal','S-hup-scan','S-init','S-auto'],participants=['Leader application caller','Candidate with pending configuration application','Old and new voters'],objects=['raftLog.applied','pendingConfIndex','Committed configuration entries','Applied voter configuration'],contexts=['Synchronous Ready/Advance mode','Early Advance while ordered state-machine application is ongoing','No reordering of actual ApplyConfChange calls'],event_paths=['Receive committed configuration in Ready; persist it; report Advance before applying it; process proposal/election while configuration call is pending; later apply entries in original order'],activity_classes=['A5','A1','A2'],behavior_ids=['B-application','B-formation','B-authority','B-auto-exit','B-recover'],fact_ids=['F-applied-notification'],supporting_behavior_ids={'B-ready':'Advance synthesizes local application response under synchronous mode','B-config':'Actual voter eligibility changes only at ApplyConfChange'},obligation_relation_kind='consumption',counterevidence=['RawNode.Advance says application has applied and saved progress; this wording may impose a stricter contract than Node.Advance.','Canonical application loops call ApplyConfChange before Advance, and asynchronous storage responses require actual application completion.','Election scans block unapplied committed configuration entries when raftLog.applied accurately tracks completed application.'],unknowns=['Does early Advance explicitly exclude configuration entries or require another ordering constraint not yet read?','Can a finite allowed schedule reach stale-configuration admission or conflicting committed entries without omitting caller work or fabricating votes?'],priority=3,disposition='needs_specific_evidence',preferred_check='source_review',trigger_rationale='Unlike the read-release asymmetry whose API permits request loss, this discrepancy concerns membership qualification for consensus decisions. Contract investigation and a small conditional execution are warranted before any safety obligation is attributed.')\n# Cite the complete selected basis required by the product contract.\nfs={f['id']:f for f in m['facts']};q['source_ids']=sorted(set(q['source_ids']+[x for i in q['behavior_ids'] for x in B[i]['source_ids']]+fs['F-applied-notification']['source_ids']))\njson.dump(m,open('map3.json','w'),indent=2)\nsources=[dict(id='S-early-advance',file='node.go',start_line=158,end_line=187,kind='interface_statement'),dict(id='S-doc-advance',file='doc.go',start_line=72,end_line=103,kind='document_statement'),dict(id='S-raw-conf-contract',file='rawnode.go',start_line=99,end_line=115,kind='interface_statement'),dict(id='S-raw-advance-contract',file='rawnode.go',start_line=478,end_line=496,kind='interface_statement'),dict(id='S-read-contract',file='node.go',start_line=216,end_line=229,kind='interface_statement')]\nsub=dict(action='continue',map_path='map3.json',map_changes=changes,question=q,sources=sources,rationale='Select a distinct safety-relevant application-notification premise while retaining the confirmed continuation result and the explicit ReadIndex retry boundary.',feedback=dict(ref_ids=['S-early-advance','S-doc-advance','S-raw-advance-contract','S-read-contract','B-application','B-authority','B-formation'],answered='ReadIndex explicitly permits lost requests and assigns retry to the caller, limiting the pending-read progress suspicion. Separately, Node.Advance and doc.go permit early notification while application is in progress, whereas RawNode.Advance describes applied-and-saved work. The same reported applied index controls pending configuration proposal admission and committed-configuration campaign scanning.',remaining=q['unknowns'],understanding='updated',rationale='Source-only selection of a new contract/history question. No new execution result or safety violation is asserted.'))\njson.dump(sub,open('submission7.json','w'),indent=2)\nPY\npython3 early_candidate.py\npython3 - <<'PY'\nimport json,subprocess\nr=json.load(open('../research.json'));p=subprocess.run(r['validation']['command']+['submission7.json'],capture_output=True,text=True);print(p.stdout);print(p.stderr)\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
